#: manual/tutorial/core.md:1
msgid "core tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:3
msgid "Write algorithms against structure, not concrete numbers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:11
msgid "This function can work over signed integers, `BigInt`, `Float`, `Double`, and future external types that implement the same traits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:14
msgid "Normalize integral inputs through `BigInt`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:22
msgid "Use `Integral::normalize` when you need one exact representation across multiple integral source types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:25
msgid "Convert integers with `lift_to`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:33
msgid "`lift_to` takes the representative of `i` in ℤ and maps it into `F` along the canonical map. Use it for constants and indices that are not mapped back. It does not preserve operations once fixed-width arithmetic wraps."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:37
msgid "A new number type only needs the canonical maps out of ℕ and ℤ:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:44
msgid "An integer type additionally implements `Integral::normalize` so that `from_integer(normalize(x)) == x`, and checks the law with `Section::of_integral().check(samples)`."
